 [Observed] Correction to the previous version of this report. Two defects, not one, and the second was in a correction. First, the earlier statement that no validated antimalarial resistance locus appears among the outliers was a filter artefact, not a biological result. The ingest filter required numalt == 1, and in Pf8 most canonical resistance codons are multiallelic or carry a spanning-deletion allele, so most of the panel was removed before any analysis ran. The cascade into the scan matrix, stated once and used in the same form throughout this report, is: 44 canonical codon-allele records genotyped by the superseded panel; 21 polymorphic in this sample; 6 of the 21 passed the ingest mask; 3 then cleared the MAF >= 0.05 cut and actually reached the scan matrix (pfdhfr C59R, pfdhfr N51I and pfaat1 S258L). pfcrt I356T, pfmdr1 D1246Y and pfcrt N326S passed the mask but were cut by MAF at the frequencies the superseded panel and the filter saw, 0.016, 0.011 and 0.008. Two of those three reproduce in the rebuilt panel (I356T 0.0158, D1246Y 0.0113); the third does not. The codon-reconstruction panel puts pfcrt N326S at 0.0005 - 1 of 1,874 called, with MalariaGEN agreeing on all 1,874 - because roughly 15 of the 16 carriers the string-matching panel counted were depth-resolved mixed calls that the new panel leaves uncalled, so the 0.008 is a fifteen-fold overstatement and is quoted here only as the value the MAF cut acted on. Second, the panel that was re-fetched to repair that artefact was itself wrong, because it identified codons by matching SnpEff ANN_HGVS_p strings: it missed pfdhps A437G entirely, reported the wrong residue at pfcrt 75, and used a pfk13 list written from memory. Its validation claim - that marginal frequencies agreed with MalariaGEN at 21 of 23 markers to within 0.03, the pfcrt 75 difference being a residue-naming matter - is withdrawn: marginal agreement does not establish per-sample agreement, and the codon-75 difference was a real error in our calls, not a naming convention. The codon-reconstruction panel reported above supersedes both and agrees with MalariaGEN call-by-call at 47,012 of 47,012 calls.
-[Interpreted] Conclusion. At the resolution these data permit, Ghanaian P. falciparum shows no differentiation this design can detect at the retained common variants. That is now the strongest claim this report carries - a statement about measured differentiation, not a demonstration of random mating, for which these data contain no direct evidence. The sensitivity analysis is the reason: the maximum pairwise FST stays within 0.00204-0.00255 across all six threshold scenarios, over 1,900 to 4,403 isolates and 3,275 to 10,876 SNPs, so the headline result is insensitive to both of the cuts that define the dataset. The companion claim does not survive the same treatment. The region-level isolation-by-distance result is significant at the published thresholds and at MAF >= 0.02, but in only 2 of 6 threshold scenarios and 4 of 7 leave-one-region-out replicates, and this report therefore no longer asserts a distance effect, weak or otherwise. What remains of the spatial signal is a single south-to-north contrast, visible in the FST ranking and in the K = 2 minor component, with no robust evidence that genetic distance scales with geographic distance. That near-panmixia is a statement about neutral common-variant structure only: neutral common-variant differentiation between zones is negligible, so a spatially sparse design is adequate for characterising neutral structure, but marker-frequency representativeness is a separate question, and the resistance panel in this analysis shows significant between-zone variation at p < 0.001 at pfdhfr, pfdhps and pfaat1. That threshold is stated because it is a selection: on the four-zone chi-square as reported, several further loci are nominally significant at p < 0.05, among them pfcrt I356T (p = 1.6 x 10 to the -3), pfmdr2 I492V (p = 0.020) and pfmdr1 N86Y (p = 0.029). Low neutral FST therefore does not license an assumption that marker frequencies are spatially representative. The pfk13 result is a genuine tested negative - no screened resistance allele occurs at any of the 23 codons, which cover the complete WHO-validated set of 14 and all 6 candidates: twenty-two are fixed at the reference residue and codon 458 is at 0.9995 on a single Asn458Asp call, which is not the validated N458Y, on 1,845 to 1,876 isolates called per codon - rather than an untested absence; it is also a statement about 2009-2021 and not about current parasite populations. The XtX set remains a ranked candidate list, of which 13 SNPs survive FDR 10% and two adjacent chromosome-11 sites - one of them the fifth-ranked SNP overall - are flagged lower confidence on ExcessHet and InbreedingCoeff grounds. All zone-level results describe a compound variable in which ecology, administrative region, collection period and sequencing study cannot be separated; that is a property of the Pf8 metadata, not of the analysis.
-[Interpreted] Robustness of these conclusions to the corrected re-runs and the final checks. The population-structure readings held on a 16.6% larger SNP panel (3,359 to 3,916 SNPs) with corrected filter ordering, a bitwise-reproducible single-threaded BayPass, seeded permutation tests and full false-discovery accounting: near-panmixia, maximum FST of approximately 0.0023, and a near-flat ADMIXTURE cross-validation curve. Three quantities moved and should not be described as unchanged: the XtX threshold (the manuscript's 8-thread run gave 11.232, the final single-threaded run gives 10.840), the outlier count (15 to 19) and the outlier gene list (11 to 13 genes). One conclusion has since been lost, and it was the report's only positive spatial finding. The threshold sensitivity analysis shows the region-level Mantel result significant in 2 of 6 scenarios, and it also withdraws the earlier claim that the result no longer depends on the censoring of negative FST pairs: of the six scenarios only the baseline clears p < 0.05 both censored and uncensored, the second significant scenario (MAF >= 0.02) giving p = 0.047 censored against p = 0.050 uncensored. Isolation by distance is retained in the report and reported with the scenarios in which it fails, but it is graded not robust, and the previous version's listing of it among the strengthened results is withdrawn. Two strengthenings stand. Near-panmixia is now supported by FST stability across 0.00204-0.00255 over all six scenarios, which is better evidence than any single run could give; and TreeMix decides m = 2 on edge p-values instead of hedging between m = 2 and m = 3. Two further checks changed nothing material. Re-running the three ordinations on the 1,900-isolate analysis set rather than the 4,905 pre-QC isolates leaves the reading intact - silhouette by zone -0.020 against -0.016 on the full set, so removing polyclonal and clonally redundant isolates does not sharpen the structure; trustworthiness 0.499 (UMAP), 0.526 (t-SNE), 0.510 (PCoA), PCoA distance correlation 0.287 against 0.322 - and the call-quality audit clears the outlier set while downgrading two chromosome-11 SNPs. The resistance panel is a different matter and was not confirmed but replaced: an external critique upheld in full showed that the marker identification method was unsound, and the panel was rebuilt from the codon up, changing the pfdhfr triple-mutant figure, adding a locus that had been missing altogether, correcting a residue call, correcting the pfk13 list, and replacing the validation argument. The population analyses are therefore a partial confirmation with two strengthened results, one lost result, two downgraded outlier SNPs and one materially revised candidate list; the resistance results are new.
